Kubernetes Debugging Cheat Sheet 🛠️

A mental model + troubleshooting guide for common Kubernetes pod and cluster issues, mapped to who is responsible and how to debug.

🔍 High-Level Responsibility Map
Problem	              Look At
Pod Pending	          Scheduler (Cluster)
ImagePullBackOff	    Node (kubelet)
CrashLoopBackOff	    Node (kubelet)
Wrong replicas	      Controller (Cluster)
API not responding	  Control Plane

_______________________________________________
1️⃣ Pod Pending → Scheduler (Cluster)
What “Pending” Means

A pod exists in the cluster but is not running.

⚠️ There are two very different kinds of Pending. This distinction is critical.

Case A: Pending + NO node assigned
kubectl get pod -o wide
    STATUS: Pending
    NODE: <none>

Why This Happens
    The scheduler cannot find a suitable node.

Common scheduler reasons:
    Not enough CPU or memory
    nodeSelector does not match any node
    Node taints without matching tolerations
    Affinity / anti-affinity rules too strict

👉 Scheduler is responsible → think cluster-level constraints.

How to Debug
    kubectl describe pod <pod-name>

Look for:
    FailedScheduling
    Insufficient cpu
    Insufficient memory

Case B: Pending + node assigned
    STATUS: Pending
    NODE: worker-1
⚠️ This is no longer the scheduler.
This is now a kubelet (node-level) problem — a common mistake.

