2️⃣ ImagePullBackOff → Node (kubelet)
What It Means
    Scheduler has already assigned the pod to a node  
    kubelet is trying to pull the container image
    Image pull is failing

Why kubelet Is Responsible
    Image pulling happens on the node

Uses:
    Node network
    DNS
    Registry credentials

Common Causes
    Wrong image name or tag
    No access to private registry
    Missing or incorrect imagePullSecrets  
    Node has no internet access

How to Debug
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
    Look for:
    Failed to pull image
    ImagePullBackOff
🧠 Node-level thinking:
    Can this node reach the registry?    
    Are credentials configured correctly?
_______________________________________________
3️⃣ CrashLoopBackOff → Node (kubelet)

What It Means
    The container:
    Starts
    Crashes
    Restarts
    Repeats endlessly

Why kubelet Is Responsible
    kubelet starts the container
    Monitors exit codes
    Restarts the container on failure

Common Causes
    Application crashes
    Wrong command or arguments
    Missing environment variables
    App exits immediately

How to Debug
    kubectl logs <pod-name>
    kubectl describe pod <pod-name>


🧠 Key Idea
    Kubernetes is working correctly.
    Your application is broken.

_________________________________________________________
4️⃣ Wrong Replicas → Controller (Cluster)
What It Means
        You asked for:
        replicas: 3
        But you see:
        Only 1 pod running
        or
        More pods than expected
        
Why Controller Is Responsible        
        Controllers:
        Continuously compare desired state vs actual state
        Create or delete pods to fix drift
        If replicas are wrong:
        Controller may not be functioning correctlz        
        OR pods can’t be created due to scheduler/node issues

How to Debug
        kubectl get deployment
        kubectl describe deployment
        kubectl get rs


Look for:
    Events
    Failed pod creation    
    ReplicaSet errors
___________________________________________________________

5️⃣ API Not Responding → Control Plane

What It Means
    kubectl get pods
    Hangs
    Or returns:
    Unable to connect to the server

Why Control Plane Is Responsible
    Everything flows through:
    kube-apiserver
    etcd
    If these are down or slow:    
    The entire cluster is effectively frozen

How to Debug
    Self-Managed Cluster
    kubectl get componentstatuses
    kubectl logs kube-apiserver

Managed Kubernetes (e.g. EKS)

    CloudWatch logs
    API server metrics  
    IAM authentication issues

🧠 SUPER IMPORTANT DEBUGGING RULE (MEMORIZE)

Always debug in this order:

    Is the API server reachable?
    Did the scheduler assign a node?
    Is kubelet running the pod?
    Is a controller fixing state drift?
______________________________________________________________________
➡️ Following this order saves hours of debugging.

🔥 Real-Life Symptom → Responsibility Mapping
    Symptom	Think
    Pending + no node	Scheduler
    Pending + node	kubelet
    Image pull error	kubelet
    CrashLoopBackOff	App / kubelet
    Wrong pod count	Controller
    kubectl broken	Control Plane
